ep by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the minimum value of the function\n\\[f(x) = \\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21} - \\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cfc9e3ff68a6e5e5b99529eac0290636a359274fda066ff93c215a7e5d048d5e"},"seconds":84.36258745193481,"seed":78844513913225390,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the minimum value of the function\n\\[f(x) = \\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21} - \\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10},\\]\nwe first need to determine the domain of the function. The expressions under the square roots must be non-negative.\n\n1. For \\(\\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21}\\):\n\\[\n-x^2 + 4x + 21 \\geq 0.\n\\]\nThis is a quadratic inequality. First, we find the roots of the equation \\(-x^2 + 4x + 21 = 0\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 84}}{-2} = \\frac{-4 \\pm 10}{-2}.\n\\]\nSo, the roots are \\(x = -3\\) and \\(x = 7\\). The quadratic \\(-x^2 + 4x + 21\\) is a downward-opening parabola, so it is non-negative between the roots \\(-3\\) and \\(7\\). Thus, the inequality \\(-x^2 + 4x + 21 \\geq 0\\) holds for \\(-3 \\leq x \\leq 7\\).\n\n2. For \\(\\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10}\\):\n\\[\n-x^2 + 3x + 10 \\geq 0.\n\\]\nSimilarly, we find the roots of the equation \\(-x^2 + 3x + 10 = 0\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 40}}{-2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm 7}{-2}.\n\\]\nSo, the roots are \\(x = -2\\) and \\(x = 5\\). The quadratic \\(-x^2 + 3x + 10\\) is a downward-opening parabola, so it is non-negative between the roots \\(-2\\) and \\(5\\). Thus, the inequality \\(-x^2 + 3x + 10 \\geq 0\\) holds for \\(-2 \\leq x \\leq 5\\).\n\nThe intersection of the two intervals \\([-3, 7]\\) and \\([-2, 5]\\) is \\([-2, 5]\\). Therefore, the domain of \\(f(x)\\) is \\([-2, 5]\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) on this interval. We can do this by evaluating \\(f(x)\\) at the endpoints and any critical points within the interval.\n\nFirst, evaluate \\(f(x)\\) at the endpoints:\n1. At \\(x = -2\\):\n\\[\nf(-2) = \\sqrt{-(-2)^2 + 4(-2) + 21} - \\sqrt{-(-2)^2 + 3(-2) + 10} = \\sqrt{-4 - 8 + 21} - \\sqrt{-4 - 6 + 10} = \\sqrt{9} - \\sqrt{0} = 3 - 0 = 3.\n\\]\n2. At \\(x = 5\\):\n\\[\nf(5) = \\sqrt{-(5)^2 + 4(5) + 21} - \\sqrt{-(5)^2 + 3(5) + 10} = \\sqrt{-25 + 20 + 21} - \\sqrt{-25 + 15 + 10} = \\sqrt{16} - \\sqrt{0} = 4 - 0 = 4.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to check if there are any critical points in the interval \\((-2, 5)\\). To do this, we find the derivative of \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21} \\right) - \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10} \\right).\n\\]\nUsing the chain rule:\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{-2x + 4}{2\\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21}} - \\frac{-2x + 3}{2\\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10}} = \\frac{-x + 2}{\\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21}} - \\frac{-x + \\frac{3}{2}}{\\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10}}.\n\\]\nSetting \\(f'(x) = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{-x + 2}{\\sqrt{-x^2 + 4x + 21}} = \\frac{-x + \\frac{3}{2}}{\\sqrt{-x^2 + 3x + 10}}.\n\\]\nThis equation is quite complex to solve analytically, so we check the values of \\(f(x)\\) at some points within the interval to see if we can find a minimum. Let's check \\(x = 0\\):\n\\[\nf(0) = \\sqrt{-0^2 + 4 \\cdot 0 + 21} - \\sqrt{-0^2 + 3 \\cdot 0 + 10} = \\sqrt{21} - \\sqrt{10}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sqrt{21} \\approx 4.583\\) and \\(\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.162\\), we have:\n\\[\nf(0) \\approx 4.583 - 3.162 = 1.421.\n\\]\nComparing the values at the endpoints and at \\(x = 0\\), we see that the minimum value is at \\(x = 0\\).\n\nThus, the minimum value of the function is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{21} - \\sqrt{10}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{21} - \\sqrt{10}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
